-- [Physical Starters] Sway and Freeze
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Sway and Freeze',
    time = 3,
    description = '<p>Start the session with Sway and Freeze to wake the body. Copy the conductor as you sway to the left then right then freeze.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Releases tension before singing</li><li>Aligns posture for freer breath and tone</li><li>Gets the whole class moving as one ensemble</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Open floor space with room to stretch and turn. Whole class. No props needed. About 3 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Copy the conductor as you sway to the left then right then freeze.</li><li>The choir to move with you.</li><li>Good for focusing their attention.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>In Sway and Freeze, the leader’s hand must move smoothly — practise the gesture silently once so voices don’t jump.</li><li>During Sway and Freeze, use one short posture cue only (‘soft knees’ or ‘long spine’) so movement stays musical.</li><li>Freeze the last shape of Sway and Freeze and check ears-over-shoulders as a class.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Do the movement without chant/song first, then add the voice.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Freeze in the final aligned posture and take one supported breath together.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '505eb8b3-f01f-43ea-933c-eb27b0b37e97';

-- [Physical Starters] Pomming
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Pomming',
    time = 3,
    description = '<p>Start the session with Pomming to wake the body. ‘Pomming’ – The conductor points to different people in the choir who when pointed to say ‘Pom’.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Releases tension before singing</li><li>Aligns posture for freer breath and tone</li><li>Gets the whole class moving as one ensemble</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Open floor space with room to stretch and turn. Whole class. No props needed. About 3 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Gather focus and watch the teacher model once in silence.</li><li>‘Pomming’ – The conductor points to different people in the choir who when pointed to say ‘Pom’.</li><li>Repeat together, then try once more with greater clarity.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>In Pomming, the leader’s hand must move smoothly — practise the gesture silently once so voices don’t jump.</li><li>During Pomming, use one short posture cue only (‘soft knees’ or ‘long spine’) so movement stays musical.</li><li>After Pomming, shake out once, then find ‘singer’s feet’ before the first note.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Do the movement without chant/song first, then add the voice.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Freeze in the final aligned posture and take one supported breath together.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '21005089-d605-4c89-9c50-438ce9474474';

-- [Physical Starters] Acting Faces Focus Points
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Acting Faces Focus Points',
    time = 3,
    description = '<p>Start the session with Acting Faces Focus Points to wake the body. Singing is just the same as acting, but the words are set to music.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Releases tension before singing</li><li>Aligns posture for freer breath and tone</li><li>Gets the whole class moving as one ensemble</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Open floor space with room to stretch and turn. Whole class. No props needed. About 3 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Singing is just the same as acting, but the words are set to music.</li><li>Ask them to use their ‘acting faces’.</li><li>Choose three focal points in the space facing the ‘audience’ – 1) stage left, 2) centre, 3) stage right.</li><li>On a clap, everyone looks at the focus point called out, showing a particular emotion eg. angry, surprised, excited, confused, sad, happy, amazed, proud etc.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>During Acting Faces Focus Points, use one short posture cue only (‘soft knees’ or ‘long spine’) so movement stays musical.</li><li>End Acting Faces Focus Points in a tall still shape for three counts so energy settles into posture.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Do the movement without chant/song first, then add the voice.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Freeze in the final aligned posture and take one supported breath together.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '52651312-78fa-4cb0-98cc-9db02328bc21';

-- [Breathing Exercises] Inflatable Ring Breathing
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Inflatable Ring Breathing',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Use Inflatable Ring Breathing to settle breath and posture before singing. Pretend you are wearing a great big inflatable rubber ring all the way around your middle.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Builds awareness of supported, low breath</li><li>Prepares steady airflow for singing phrases</li><li>Calms the class and focuses listening before repertoire</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Stand or sit tall with free shoulders. Whole class. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Pretend you are wearing a great big inflatable rubber ring all the way around your middle.</li><li>What you’re going to do is to push the ring out as you breathe in over the count of 4 without making a single noise.</li><li>Then breathe out very quietly over a count of 4.</li><li>Keep the shoulders completely relaxed and down.</li><li>Your lungs will work without you having to use your shoulders.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>In Inflatable Ring Breathing, cue ‘fill from the bottom’ and watch shoulders: if they lift, mime pushing the ring/balloon lower.</li><li>In Inflatable Ring Breathing, pause after the first full cycle and ask one pupil what they felt move — name that sensation.</li><li>Repeat Inflatable Ring Breathing once seated if standing posture collapsed — compare the two sensations.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Teacher models one slow cycle; class joins on the out-breath only.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Transfer the same breath feeling into the opening line of today’s repertoire.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '9dec4092-ad66-49e7-ae94-b326c26498c5';

-- [Breathing Exercises] Hands Under Rib Cage
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Hands Under Rib Cage',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Use Hands Under Rib Cage to settle breath and posture before singing. Place your hands underneath your rib cage, breathe in and out in your own time but try to get the sensation of breathing under where your hands are.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Builds awareness of supported, low breath</li><li>Prepares steady airflow for singing phrases</li><li>Links posture and breathing muscles for clearer tone</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Stand or sit tall with free shoulders. Whole class. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Place your hands underneath your rib cage, breathe in and out in your own time but try to get the sensation of breathing under where your hands are.</li><li>Try to breathe deeply.</li><li>Repeat but with their hands together as in prayer.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>During Hands Under Rib Cage, hands on ribs/tummy stay light — expansion should be sideways/forwards, not a heaved chest.</li><li>In Hands Under Rib Cage, pause after the first full cycle and ask one pupil what they felt move — name that sensation.</li><li>After Hands Under Rib Cage, ask ‘where did the air go?’ and point to ribs/tummy before repertoire.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Teacher models one slow cycle; class joins on the out-breath only.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Transfer the same breath feeling into the opening line of today’s repertoire.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '15459568-aa41-4b6f-9935-1b6635e401f8';

-- [Breathing Exercises] Finger Candles
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Finger Candles',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Use Finger Candles to settle breath and posture before singing. Hold out one hand in front of you, palm facing towards you.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Builds awareness of supported, low breath</li><li>Prepares steady airflow for singing phrases</li><li>Practises controlled release of air without strain</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Stand or sit tall with free shoulders. Whole class. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Hold out one hand in front of you, palm facing towards you.</li><li>Imagine that each finger is a candle.</li><li>Blow out each candle in turn and then on the last candle blow out any remaining breath.</li><li>Repeat with the other hand under your belly button.</li><li>Repeat with the following sounds: ff; ff ff ff ff ; ssh ssh ssh ssh ssh.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>For Finger Candles, keep the imaginary flame small — if air blasts, reset to a gentle stream on one finger.</li><li>During Finger Candles, hands on ribs/tummy stay light — expansion should be sideways/forwards, not a heaved chest.</li><li>Keep the airflow in Finger Candles quiet and even; stop if throats tighten and rematch on a softer ‘sss’.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Teacher models one slow cycle; class joins on the out-breath only.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Transfer the same breath feeling into the opening line of today’s repertoire.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '6f715661-d739-4234-b40f-a4ad36058d7e';

-- [Breathing Exercises] Ssh Shower Countdowns
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Ssh Shower Countdowns',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Use Ssh Shower Countdowns to settle breath and posture before singing. Say ‘Ssh, ssh, ssh, sshhhhh’ in rhythm.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Builds awareness of supported, low breath</li><li>Prepares steady airflow for singing phrases</li><li>Practises controlled release of air without strain</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Stand or sit tall with free shoulders. Whole class. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Say ‘Ssh, ssh, ssh, sshhhhh’ in rhythm.</li><li>Then incorporate it giving yourself a shower.</li><li>Set the challenge of while the water is running down – you’re going to be the water going down using the ‘Ssh’ sound over a count of 4.</li><li>You have that count of 4 to wash all over and then return to rubbing your hands in front of you.</li><li>Then repeat it over a count of 3.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>Keep the airflow in Ssh Shower Countdowns quiet and even; stop if throats tighten and rematch on a softer ‘sss’.</li><li>In Ssh Shower Countdowns, pause after the first full cycle and ask one pupil what they felt move — name that sensation.</li><li>After Ssh Shower Countdowns, ask ‘where did the air go?’ and point to ribs/tummy before repertoire.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Teacher models one slow cycle; class joins on the out-breath only.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Transfer the same breath feeling into the opening line of today’s repertoire.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = 'd65e3fa3-9a77-4352-b79b-16526d4b7201';

-- [Breathing Exercises] Single Flame Puffs
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Single Flame Puffs',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Use Single Flame Puffs to settle breath and posture before singing. Stretch one arm out in front of them with one finger pointing up, imagining there is a flame on the end of it.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Builds awareness of supported, low breath</li><li>Prepares steady airflow for singing phrases</li><li>Practises controlled release of air without strain</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Stand or sit tall with free shoulders. Whole class. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Stretch one arm out in front of them with one finger pointing up, imagining there is a flame on the end of it.</li><li>Without bending towards it they are to try and blow it out with short puffs.</li><li>Can they feel the air on their finger and their diaphragm muscle working?</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>For Single Flame Puffs, keep the imaginary flame small — if air blasts, reset to a gentle stream on one finger.</li><li>During Single Flame Puffs, hands on ribs/tummy stay light — expansion should be sideways/forwards, not a heaved chest.</li><li>After Single Flame Puffs, ask ‘where did the air go?’ and point to ribs/tummy before repertoire.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Teacher models one slow cycle; class joins on the out-breath only.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Transfer the same breath feeling into the opening line of today’s repertoire.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '347a0001-a8ec-4af3-8dbc-be0b02498b25';

-- [Breathing Exercises] 4-4-4-4 Open Throat Breath
UPDATE activities
SET activity = '4-4-4-4 Open Throat Breath',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Use 4-4-4-4 Open Throat Breath to settle breath and posture before singing. Shoulders should never rise when inhaling.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Builds awareness of supported, low breath</li><li>Prepares steady airflow for singing phrases</li><li>Links posture and breathing muscles for clearer tone</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Stand or sit tall with free shoulders. Whole class. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Shoulders should never rise when inhaling.</li><li>Imagine they are breathing into their tummies/belly button ie.</li><li>Releasing the diaphragm to allow air into the lungs.</li><li>Breathe in with an open throat, as if in a slight yawn.</li><li>With hands by your sides, feel you are expanding out at the sides and from the back as well as the front.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>During 4-4-4-4 Open Throat Breath, hands on ribs/tummy stay light — expansion should be sideways/forwards, not a heaved chest.</li><li>In 4-4-4-4 Open Throat Breath, pause after the first full cycle and ask one pupil what they felt move — name that sensation.</li><li>After 4-4-4-4 Open Throat Breath, ask ‘where did the air go?’ and point to ribs/tummy before repertoire.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Teacher models one slow cycle; class joins on the out-breath only.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Transfer the same breath feeling into the opening line of today’s repertoire.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = 'ca27cf6a-63fe-4d3f-a363-b95e9ee602dc';

-- [Breathing Exercises] Spaghetti Voice Projection
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Spaghetti Voice Projection',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Use Spaghetti Voice Projection to settle breath and posture before singing. To help the children to visualise how to project their singing voice ask them to imagine a long piece of spaghetti or chewing gum being pulled out….</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Builds awareness of supported, low breath</li><li>Prepares steady airflow for singing phrases</li><li>Calms the class and focuses listening before repertoire</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Stand or sit tall with free shoulders. Whole class. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Gather focus and watch the teacher model once in silence.</li><li>To help the children to visualise how to project their singing voice ask them to imagine a long piece of spaghetti or chewing gum being pulled out of the mouth or a laser beam shining as they sing ‘Sing this note’.</li><li>Repeat together, then try once more with greater clarity.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>In Spaghetti Voice Projection, keep the chew huge and silent for two beats before adding voice — floppy face, not clenched jaw.</li><li>In Spaghetti Voice Projection, pause after the first full cycle and ask one pupil what they felt move — name that sensation.</li><li>Leave Spaghetti Voice Projection on a silent shared inhale — then speak the first lyric on that same breath feeling.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Teacher models one slow cycle; class joins on the out-breath only.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Transfer the same breath feeling into the opening line of today’s repertoire.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = 'eae4667f-d0b7-4c95-8772-43210c410ad6';

-- [Breathing Exercises] Laughing Name Trace
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Laughing Name Trace',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Use Laughing Name Trace to settle breath and posture before singing. Say the word ‘laughing’ and then hold on to the ‘ing’ whilst pencilling their full name with an imaginary pencil in the air.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Builds awareness of supported, low breath</li><li>Prepares steady airflow for singing phrases</li><li>Calms the class and focuses listening before repertoire</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Stand or sit tall with free shoulders. Whole class. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Gather focus and watch the teacher model once in silence.</li><li>Say the word ‘laughing’ and then hold on to the ‘ing’ whilst pencilling their full name with an imaginary pencil in the air.</li><li>Repeat together, then try once more with greater clarity.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>Keep theatrical laughs in Laughing Name Trace supported from the middle — fun, not scraped from the throat.</li><li>In Laughing Name Trace, pause after the first full cycle and ask one pupil what they felt move — name that sensation.</li><li>After Laughing Name Trace, ask ‘where did the air go?’ and point to ribs/tummy before repertoire.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Teacher models one slow cycle; class joins on the out-breath only.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Transfer the same breath feeling into the opening line of today’s repertoire.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = 'f2f0cabb-cb10-41fd-a49b-e20b51f728fb';

-- [Breathing Exercises] Breath Awareness and Diaphragm
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Breath Awareness and Diaphragm',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Use Breath Awareness and Diaphragm to settle breath and posture before singing. Breathe normally.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Builds awareness of supported, low breath</li><li>Prepares steady airflow for singing phrases</li><li>Links posture and breathing muscles for clearer tone</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Stand or sit tall with free shoulders. Whole class. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Breathe normally.</li><li>Become aware of the muscles you use as you inhale and exhale.</li><li>Repeat but breathing deeper, using only the same muscles.</li><li>Keep shoulders relaxed.</li><li>Your diaphragm (the dome-shaped muscle that separates the lungs from your abdomen) and the muscles below it are the basis of your air.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>During Breath Awareness and Diaphragm, hands on ribs/tummy stay light — expansion should be sideways/forwards, not a heaved chest.</li><li>In Breath Awareness and Diaphragm, pause after the first full cycle and ask one pupil what they felt move — name that sensation.</li><li>Leave Breath Awareness and Diaphragm on a silent shared inhale — then speak the first lyric on that same breath feeling.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Teacher models one slow cycle; class joins on the out-breath only.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Transfer the same breath feeling into the opening line of today’s repertoire.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '5cc2222f-64e4-4284-be27-08ddfb7319c5';

-- [Breathing Exercises] Clasp and Stretch Breath
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Clasp and Stretch Breath',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Use Clasp and Stretch Breath to settle breath and posture before singing. Check your posture, clasp your hands together in front of you with palms outward.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Builds awareness of supported, low breath</li><li>Prepares steady airflow for singing phrases</li><li>Links posture and breathing muscles for clearer tone</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Stand or sit tall with free shoulders. Whole class. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Check your posture, clasp your hands together in front of you with palms outward.</li><li>As you breathe in, bring the hands up slowly, switching the direction of the clasp.</li><li>As you breathe out stretch the arms out.</li><li>Make sure you are relaxed with no tension anywhere.</li><li>Close your eyes and do it in your own space.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>Model the Clasp and Stretch Breath posture check once, then let pairs self-check: ears over shoulders, soft knees.</li><li>In Clasp and Stretch Breath, pause after the first full cycle and ask one pupil what they felt move — name that sensation.</li><li>After Clasp and Stretch Breath, ask ‘where did the air go?’ and point to ribs/tummy before repertoire.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Teacher models one slow cycle; class joins on the out-breath only.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Transfer the same breath feeling into the opening line of today’s repertoire.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = 'cf004b13-8d35-4960-819d-5c269ed6e597';

-- [Breathing Exercises] Candle Support Flow
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Candle Support Flow',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Use Candle Support Flow to settle breath and posture before singing. One function of your support is to create a steady air pressure on which to base a steady, consistent sound.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Builds awareness of supported, low breath</li><li>Prepares steady airflow for singing phrases</li><li>Practises controlled release of air without strain</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Stand or sit tall with free shoulders. Whole class. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>One function of your support is to create a steady air pressure on which to base a steady, consistent sound.</li><li>Imagine a candle in front of your mouth and blow it out very gently, using a steady flow of air.</li><li>Blow it out more firmly, and feel the muscle action lower down in your back, sides and front.</li><li>Next, try saying, ‘mmmm’ rising and falling in pitch, as if smelling something wonderful.</li><li>Repeat, but this time gain more resonance and projection by adding pressure as if blowing the candle out.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>For Candle Support Flow, keep the imaginary flame small — if air blasts, reset to a gentle stream on one finger.</li><li>In Candle Support Flow, pause after the first full cycle and ask one pupil what they felt move — name that sensation.</li><li>Repeat Candle Support Flow once seated if standing posture collapsed — compare the two sensations.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Teacher models one slow cycle; class joins on the out-breath only.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Transfer the same breath feeling into the opening line of today’s repertoire.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '0dab2604-6b01-41e6-a451-75ceb4d6d5ff';

-- [Breathing Exercises] Reflex Breath Reset
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Reflex Breath Reset',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Use Reflex Breath Reset to settle breath and posture before singing. Completely exhale, maintaining a good posture, followed by a ‘reflex’ breath in, letting go of tummy muscles.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Builds awareness of supported, low breath</li><li>Prepares steady airflow for singing phrases</li><li>Links posture and breathing muscles for clearer tone</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Stand or sit tall with free shoulders. Whole class. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Gather focus and watch the teacher model once in silence.</li><li>Completely exhale, maintaining a good posture, followed by a ‘reflex’ breath in, letting go of tummy muscles.</li><li>Repeat together, then try once more with greater clarity.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>During Reflex Breath Reset, hands on ribs/tummy stay light — expansion should be sideways/forwards, not a heaved chest.</li><li>Model the Reflex Breath Reset posture check once, then let pairs self-check: ears over shoulders, soft knees.</li><li>Leave Reflex Breath Reset on a silent shared inhale — then speak the first lyric on that same breath feeling.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Teacher models one slow cycle; class joins on the out-breath only.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Transfer the same breath feeling into the opening line of today’s repertoire.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = 'e4ededdf-95dc-47f8-ad37-b850b6a5bca0';

-- [Breathing Exercises] Sss Ff Ssh Pulses
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Sss Ff Ssh Pulses',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Use Sss Ff Ssh Pulses to settle breath and posture before singing. Completely exhale to an ‘ss’ then ‘ff’ then ‘ssh’ sound.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Builds awareness of supported, low breath</li><li>Prepares steady airflow for singing phrases</li><li>Practises controlled release of air without strain</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Stand or sit tall with free shoulders. Whole class. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Completely exhale to an ‘ss’ then ‘ff’ then ‘ssh’ sound.</li><li>Change this to sounds which are voices eg. ‘zz’, ‘vv’ and ‘zssosh’ as in the ‘s’ of ‘vision’.</li><li>Add diaphragmatic pulsing, keeping the sound continuous.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>Keep the airflow in Sss Ff Ssh Pulses quiet and even; stop if throats tighten and rematch on a softer ‘sss’.</li><li>In Sss Ff Ssh Pulses, pause after the first full cycle and ask one pupil what they felt move — name that sensation.</li><li>After Sss Ff Ssh Pulses, ask ‘where did the air go?’ and point to ribs/tummy before repertoire.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Teacher models one slow cycle; class joins on the out-breath only.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Transfer the same breath feeling into the opening line of today’s repertoire.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '54c00712-724b-400a-b4c3-1eae948f8caa';

-- [Breathing Exercises] Steam Train Exercise
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Steam Train Exercise',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Use Steam Train Exercise to settle breath and posture before singing. Carry out the ‘Steam Train exercise’ say the sound ‘ch ch ch ch ch’ as if representing the sound of a train and the carry out a long ‘sssss’….</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Builds awareness of supported, low breath</li><li>Prepares steady airflow for singing phrases</li><li>Calms the class and focuses listening before repertoire</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Stand or sit tall with free shoulders. Whole class. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Carry out the ‘Steam Train exercise’ say the sound ‘ch ch ch ch ch’ as if representing the sound of a train and the carry out a long ‘sssss’ exhaling completely on the sss.</li><li>Take a reflex breath in adding a high ‘Woowoo’ for the train whistle at the end.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>Keep the airflow in Steam Train Exercise quiet and even; stop if throats tighten and rematch on a softer ‘sss’.</li><li>In Steam Train Exercise, pause after the first full cycle and ask one pupil what they felt move — name that sensation.</li><li>Repeat Steam Train Exercise once seated if standing posture collapsed — compare the two sensations.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Teacher models one slow cycle; class joins on the out-breath only.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Transfer the same breath feeling into the opening line of today’s repertoire.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = 'a040f604-1c1d-4d18-9203-822187c917bd';

-- [Breathing Exercises] Engine Room Breathing
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Engine Room Breathing',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Use Engine Room Breathing to settle breath and posture before singing. Explore their breathing.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Builds awareness of supported, low breath</li><li>Prepares steady airflow for singing phrases</li><li>Practises controlled release of air without strain</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Stand or sit tall with free shoulders. Whole class. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Explore their breathing.</li><li>All stand still, close eyes, and wait until they need to breathe.</li><li>Where does the in-breath start?</li><li>In their tummy.</li><li>Placing a hand on their tummy, feel the breath as it rises under the hand on the next in-breath.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>In Engine Room Breathing, cue ‘fill from the bottom’ and watch shoulders: if they lift, mime pushing the ring/balloon lower.</li><li>During Engine Room Breathing, hands on ribs/tummy stay light — expansion should be sideways/forwards, not a heaved chest.</li><li>Repeat Engine Room Breathing once seated if standing posture collapsed — compare the two sensations.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Teacher models one slow cycle; class joins on the out-breath only.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Transfer the same breath feeling into the opening line of today’s repertoire.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '3d5af90c-7997-4bf0-863e-2a3f62fc3822';

-- [Breathing Exercises] Gentle Ha to Laugh
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Gentle Ha to Laugh',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Use Gentle Ha to Laugh to settle breath and posture before singing. Finally, try gently making a very short ‘ha’ on cue.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Builds awareness of supported, low breath</li><li>Prepares steady airflow for singing phrases</li><li>Links posture and breathing muscles for clearer tone</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Stand or sit tall with free shoulders. Whole class. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Finally, try gently making a very short ‘ha’ on cue.</li><li>Use no effort, as if a drop of water is hitting your diaphragm and spreading out.</li><li>Next, try two gentle ones – ‘ha-ha’.</li><li>Finally, laugh, and feel your diaphragm kicking in.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>During Gentle Ha to Laugh, hands on ribs/tummy stay light — expansion should be sideways/forwards, not a heaved chest.</li><li>Keep theatrical laughs in Gentle Ha to Laugh supported from the middle — fun, not scraped from the throat.</li><li>Leave Gentle Ha to Laugh on a silent shared inhale — then speak the first lyric on that same breath feeling.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Teacher models one slow cycle; class joins on the out-breath only.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Transfer the same breath feeling into the opening line of today’s repertoire.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '225cb2a7-c666-4d32-8a90-590b0b2a4805';

-- [Breathing Exercises] Breathing Muscle Stretch
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Breathing Muscle Stretch',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Use Breathing Muscle Stretch to settle breath and posture before singing. Stretch their breathing muscles by stretching the right arm into the air, breathe out then sip in several times before blowing out.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Builds awareness of supported, low breath</li><li>Prepares steady airflow for singing phrases</li><li>Practises controlled release of air without strain</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Stand or sit tall with free shoulders. Whole class. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Stretch their breathing muscles by stretching the right arm into the air, breathe out then sip in several times before blowing out.</li><li>Repeat with the other arm.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>In Breathing Muscle Stretch, pause after the first full cycle and ask one pupil what they felt move — name that sensation.</li><li>Repeat Breathing Muscle Stretch once seated if standing posture collapsed — compare the two sensations.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Teacher models one slow cycle; class joins on the out-breath only.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Transfer the same breath feeling into the opening line of today’s repertoire.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '3b8704c8-73c6-4921-823f-35a3ef6868ba';

-- [Breathing Exercises] Birthday Candles Control
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Birthday Candles Control',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Use Birthday Candles Control to settle breath and posture before singing. Take a breath in to the steady count of 3: hold this breath for 3 counts then sharply blow out (the number of times of their age) separate candles….</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Builds awareness of supported, low breath</li><li>Prepares steady airflow for singing phrases</li><li>Practises controlled release of air without strain</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Stand or sit tall with free shoulders. Whole class. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Take a breath in to the steady count of 3: hold this breath for 3 counts then sharply blow out (the number of times of their age) separate candles on a birthday cake (pupils to place their hand on their diaphragm so they can feel it kicking as they blow) then let the rest of the breath out as steadily as possible until it is all gone.</li><li>The aim of this exercise is to show the pupil what it feels like to try and control their breathing.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>For Birthday Candles Control, keep the imaginary flame small — if air blasts, reset to a gentle stream on one finger.</li><li>During Birthday Candles Control, hands on ribs/tummy stay light — expansion should be sideways/forwards, not a heaved chest.</li><li>Leave Birthday Candles Control on a silent shared inhale — then speak the first lyric on that same breath feeling.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Teacher models one slow cycle; class joins on the out-breath only.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Transfer the same breath feeling into the opening line of today’s repertoire.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '65f288e8-afed-459b-934f-6c74e9230cba';

-- [Breathing Exercises] Theatrical Laughs
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Theatrical Laughs',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Use Theatrical Laughs to settle breath and posture before singing. Laugh in an exaggerated, theatrical way with their hands placed on their tummies and notice what happens.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Builds awareness of supported, low breath</li><li>Prepares steady airflow for singing phrases</li><li>Calms the class and focuses listening before repertoire</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Stand or sit tall with free shoulders. Whole class. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Laugh in an exaggerated, theatrical way with their hands placed on their tummies and notice what happens.</li><li>Be as outrageous as possible with their laughs - think pantomime dame, wicked witch, jolly giant or evil ogre.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>Keep theatrical laughs in Theatrical Laughs supported from the middle — fun, not scraped from the throat.</li><li>Alternate sound effects in Theatrical Laughs with a recovery breath so the class doesn’t get dizzy.</li><li>After Theatrical Laughs, ask ‘where did the air go?’ and point to ribs/tummy before repertoire.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Teacher models one slow cycle; class joins on the out-breath only.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Add a longer counted exhale or link straight into the first phrase of a song.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Transfer the same breath feeling into the opening line of today’s repertoire.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '7da43fcf-9ed8-4a66-9c9e-adb1e622341d';

-- [Breathing Exercises] Paper Aeroplane Ssshh
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Paper Aeroplane Ssshh',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Use Paper Aeroplane Ssshh to settle breath and posture before singing. Imagine you have a paper aeroplane which you are going throw across the room and it’s going to make this sound – Sssshh.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Builds awareness of supported, low breath</li><li>Prepares steady airflow for singing phrases</li><li>Practises controlled release of air without strain</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Stand or sit tall with free shoulders. Whole class. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Imagine you have a paper aeroplane which you are going throw across the room and it’s going to make this sound – Sssshh.</li><li>Choose somewhere else in the room to throw it.</li><li>Now flick it three times before taking aim – Ssh, Ssh, Ssh, Ssshhh.</li><li>Repeat without the hand action.</li><li>Ask the group to say what is happening physically inside them.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>Keep the airflow in Paper Aeroplane Ssshh quiet and even; stop if throats tighten and rematch on a softer ‘sss’.</li><li>In Paper Aeroplane Ssshh, pause after the first full cycle and ask one pupil what they felt move — name that sensation.</li><li>After Paper Aeroplane Ssshh, ask ‘where did the air go?’ and point to ribs/tummy before repertoire.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Teacher models one slow cycle; class joins on the out-breath only.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Transfer the same breath feeling into the opening line of today’s repertoire.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '1cff8634-30ce-475d-9024-08344fb14165';

-- [Breathing Exercises] Silent Drink of Air
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Silent Drink of Air',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Use Silent Drink of Air to settle breath and posture before singing. Explain that the fuel for singing is air.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Builds awareness of supported, low breath</li><li>Prepares steady airflow for singing phrases</li><li>Calms the class and focuses listening before repertoire</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Stand or sit tall with free shoulders. Whole class. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Explain that the fuel for singing is air.</li><li>Ask the children to put two fingers into their mouth to feel the shape that their mouth should be in when taking in air.</li><li>Put their hand around an imaginary glass and take a silent drink of air through an open mouth.</li><li>They should not tip their heads back or raise their shoulders.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>In Silent Drink of Air, pause after the first full cycle and ask one pupil what they felt move — name that sensation.</li><li>After Silent Drink of Air, ask ‘where did the air go?’ and point to ribs/tummy before repertoire.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Teacher models one slow cycle; class joins on the out-breath only.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Transfer the same breath feeling into the opening line of today’s repertoire.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '164496a6-849c-4596-b62e-4fdc05c3f69a';

-- [Breathing Exercises] Short Burst Candle Blows
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Short Burst Candle Blows',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Use Short Burst Candle Blows to settle breath and posture before singing. Imagine blowing out a candle in short bursts, eg.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Builds awareness of supported, low breath</li><li>Prepares steady airflow for singing phrases</li><li>Practises controlled release of air without strain</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Stand or sit tall with free shoulders. Whole class. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Gather focus and watch the teacher model once in silence.</li><li>Imagine blowing out a candle in short bursts, eg. fff fff fff.</li><li>Repeat together, then try once more with greater clarity.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>For Short Burst Candle Blows, keep the imaginary flame small — if air blasts, reset to a gentle stream on one finger.</li><li>In Short Burst Candle Blows, pause after the first full cycle and ask one pupil what they felt move — name that sensation.</li><li>After Short Burst Candle Blows, ask ‘where did the air go?’ and point to ribs/tummy before repertoire.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Teacher models one slow cycle; class joins on the out-breath only.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Transfer the same breath feeling into the opening line of today’s repertoire.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = 'fa9a1c3e-29b2-4eef-b324-9b5d514d25e6';

-- [Breathing Exercises] Bee Breath Counts
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Bee Breath Counts',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Use Bee Breath Counts to settle breath and posture before singing. Exhale on ‘sss’ or ‘zzz’ sounds, like a buzzing bee.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Builds awareness of supported, low breath</li><li>Prepares steady airflow for singing phrases</li><li>Calms the class and focuses listening before repertoire</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Stand or sit tall with free shoulders. Whole class. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Exhale on ‘sss’ or ‘zzz’ sounds, like a buzzing bee.</li><li>Try short separate breaths to ‘sss’ counting 1,2,3,4 and then a long ‘sss’ on 5,6,7,8.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>Keep the airflow in Bee Breath Counts quiet and even; stop if throats tighten and rematch on a softer ‘sss’.</li><li>In Bee Breath Counts, pause after the first full cycle and ask one pupil what they felt move — name that sensation.</li><li>Leave Bee Breath Counts on a silent shared inhale — then speak the first lyric on that same breath feeling.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Teacher models one slow cycle; class joins on the out-breath only.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Transfer the same breath feeling into the opening line of today’s repertoire.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = 'fdc33b62-aff9-48ec-8c8d-33ebec54d3e2';

-- [Breathing Exercises] Tummy Buzz
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Tummy Buzz',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Use Tummy Buzz to settle breath and posture before singing. Put a hand on your tummy.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Builds awareness of supported, low breath</li><li>Prepares steady airflow for singing phrases</li><li>Links posture and breathing muscles for clearer tone</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Stand or sit tall with free shoulders. Whole class. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Put a hand on your tummy.</li><li>When you inhale it will expand slightly.</li><li>When you exhale it will contract.</li><li>Exhale on a zzz.</li><li>Buzz like a bee.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>During Tummy Buzz, hands on ribs/tummy stay light — expansion should be sideways/forwards, not a heaved chest.</li><li>Keep the airflow in Tummy Buzz quiet and even; stop if throats tighten and rematch on a softer ‘sss’.</li><li>Leave Tummy Buzz on a silent shared inhale — then speak the first lyric on that same breath feeling.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Teacher models one slow cycle; class joins on the out-breath only.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Add a longer counted exhale or link straight into the first phrase of a song.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Transfer the same breath feeling into the opening line of today’s repertoire.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '35ced2a4-a13c-4152-839b-b4ca8e4aa787';

-- [Breathing Exercises] Breath Support Sound Patterns
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Breath Support Sound Patterns',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Use Breath Support Sound Patterns to settle breath and posture before singing. Try putting several sounds together ‘ff’, ‘tt’, ‘ff’, ‘tt’ or ‘ss’, ‘hh’, ‘ff’, ‘tt’.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Builds awareness of supported, low breath</li><li>Prepares steady airflow for singing phrases</li><li>Links posture and breathing muscles for clearer tone</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Stand or sit tall with free shoulders. Whole class. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Try putting several sounds together ‘ff’, ‘tt’, ‘ff’, ‘tt’ or ‘ss’, ‘hh’, ‘ff’, ‘tt’.</li><li>Put their hands on their tummy and feel the muscles kicking to help them make the sounds.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>During Breath Support Sound Patterns, hands on ribs/tummy stay light — expansion should be sideways/forwards, not a heaved chest.</li><li>In Breath Support Sound Patterns, pause after the first full cycle and ask one pupil what they felt move — name that sensation.</li><li>Leave Breath Support Sound Patterns on a silent shared inhale — then speak the first lyric on that same breath feeling.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Teacher models one slow cycle; class joins on the out-breath only.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Transfer the same breath feeling into the opening line of today’s repertoire.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '1524e115-7894-453e-a851-0ddaf90152ce';

-- [Breathing Exercises] 8- and 16-Beat Candle
UPDATE activities
SET activity = '8- and 16-Beat Candle',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Use 8- and 16-Beat Candle to settle breath and posture before singing. Hold out a finger as an imaginary candle and blow a gentle breath on to it for 8 beats and then 16 beats.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Builds awareness of supported, low breath</li><li>Prepares steady airflow for singing phrases</li><li>Practises controlled release of air without strain</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Stand or sit tall with free shoulders. Whole class. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Gather focus and watch the teacher model once in silence.</li><li>Hold out a finger as an imaginary candle and blow a gentle breath on to it for 8 beats and then 16 beats.</li><li>Repeat together, then try once more with greater clarity.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>For 8- and 16-Beat Candle, keep the imaginary flame small — if air blasts, reset to a gentle stream on one finger.</li><li>In 8- and 16-Beat Candle, pause after the first full cycle and ask one pupil what they felt move — name that sensation.</li><li>After 8- and 16-Beat Candle, ask ‘where did the air go?’ and point to ribs/tummy before repertoire.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Teacher models one slow cycle; class joins on the out-breath only.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Transfer the same breath feeling into the opening line of today’s repertoire.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '6bf0f2bd-f125-4ab3-bb08-c981b89b137c';

