    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Warm the face and diction with Zinganingningning. Practice saying the words ‘Zinganingningning’ quickly then sing down a scale.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Wakes facial muscles for clear diction</li><li>Improves articulation without forcing the voice</li><li>Makes warm-up playful so diction feels low-stakes</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Standing or seated circle so everyone can see mouths. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Gather focus and watch the teacher model once in silence.</li><li>Practice saying the words ‘Zinganingningning’ quickly then sing down a scale.</li><li>Repeat together, then try once more with greater clarity.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>Give a clear starting pitch each repeat of Zinganingningning; if top notes shout, narrow to a gentle ‘ng’ slide first.</li><li>For Zinganingningning, contrast a muddy version with a crystal-clear version so the diction target is obvious.</li><li>After Zinganingningning, spot one consonant that still smudges and drill only that word.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Speak the pattern slowly in unison before any pitch or speed challenge.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Drop the warm-up consonants into a tricky lyric from the song.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '593f1e38-3bb0-47b7-b3b0-000acc50c0bb';

-- [Facial Warm-Ups / Diction] Tongue Workout
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Tongue Workout',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Warm the face and diction with Tongue Workout. Explain that the tongue is a large, strong muscle that is very important for both singing and speaking.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Wakes facial muscles for clear diction</li><li>Improves articulation without forcing the voice</li><li>Strengthens tongue agility for consonants</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Standing or seated circle so everyone can see mouths. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Explain that the tongue is a large, strong muscle that is very important for both singing and speaking.</li><li>Like any muscle it needs a good workout to function at its best.</li><li>The tongue needs to be free and nimble so that words are clear.</li><li>Stick out the tongue as far as it will go.</li><li>Can they touch their nose with their tongue?</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>For Tongue Workout, exaggerate tip-of-tongue consonants slowly first; only speed up when every syllable stays clear.</li><li>For Tongue Workout, contrast a muddy version with a crystal-clear version so the diction target is obvious.</li><li>Close Tongue Workout by mouthing the pattern silently, then add voice — clarity first.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Speak the pattern slowly in unison before any pitch or speed challenge.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Drop the warm-up consonants into a tricky lyric from the song.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '3e7c28f1-fb8a-4e8f-8974-d7d50d0350cd';

-- [Facial Warm-Ups / Diction] Squidgy Sausage Roll
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Squidgy Sausage Roll',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Warm the face and diction with Squidgy Sausage Roll. Sing the phrase ‘One squidgy, squashy, smelly, sausage roll’ on a monotone, singing one note higher on each repeat.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Wakes facial muscles for clear diction</li><li>Improves articulation without forcing the voice</li><li>Loosens lips for freer onset and buzz</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Standing or seated circle so everyone can see mouths. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Sing the phrase ‘One squidgy, squashy, smelly, sausage roll’ on a monotone, singing one note higher on each repeat.</li><li>Then sing with a very miserable, fed up face, followed by a smiley, cheerful face.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>For Squidgy Sausage Roll, contrast a muddy version with a crystal-clear version so the diction target is obvious.</li><li>Close Squidgy Sausage Roll by mouthing the pattern silently, then add voice — clarity first.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Speak the pattern slowly in unison before any pitch or speed challenge.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Drop the warm-up consonants into a tricky lyric from the song.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = 'ba073ba0-08a6-4935-ac00-815a4a6df617';

-- [Facial Warm-Ups / Diction] Chew Blue Glue / Pa Car / Bees
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Chew Blue Glue / Pa Car / Bees',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Warm the face and diction with Chew Blue Glue / Pa Car / Bees. Sing the following on a monotone to practise good vowel sounds: ‘Do you chew blue glue?’ (‘oo’); ‘Ah, there’s Pa’s car!’ (‘ah’); ‘We three bees need….</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Wakes facial muscles for clear diction</li><li>Improves articulation without forcing the voice</li><li>Makes warm-up playful so diction feels low-stakes</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Standing or seated circle so everyone can see mouths. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Sing the following on a monotone to practise good vowel sounds: ‘Do you chew blue glue?’ (‘oo’).</li><li>‘Ah, there’s Pa’s car!’ (‘ah’).</li><li>‘We three bees need trees!’ (‘ee’).</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>In Chew Blue Glue / Pa Car / Bees, keep the chew huge and silent for two beats before adding voice — floppy face, not clenched jaw.</li><li>On vowel changes in Chew Blue Glue / Pa Car / Bees, keep the jaw released; spot-check one row if sounds collapse into a muffled schwa.</li><li>Close Chew Blue Glue / Pa Car / Bees by mouthing the pattern silently, then add voice — clarity first.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Speak the pattern slowly in unison before any pitch or speed challenge.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Drop the warm-up consonants into a tricky lyric from the song.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '292e6a52-fcd8-4941-ae29-c1ef9ed60243';

-- [Facial Warm-Ups / Diction] Pss Tucka Tss Trio
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Pss Tucka Tss Trio',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Warm the face and diction with Pss Tucka Tss Trio. Practised speaking the sounds: 1) pss pss f pss pss f 2) tucka tucka tick tick 3) tss---------- tucka ta and divided into 3 groups to put each part….</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Wakes facial muscles for clear diction</li><li>Improves articulation without forcing the voice</li><li>Makes warm-up playful so diction feels low-stakes</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Standing or seated circle so everyone can see mouths. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Gather focus and watch the teacher model once in silence.</li><li>Practised speaking the sounds: 1) pss pss f pss pss f 2) tucka tucka tick tick 3) tss---------- tucka ta and divided into 3 groups to put each part together.</li><li>Repeat together, then try once more with greater clarity.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>For Pss Tucka Tss Trio, contrast a muddy version with a crystal-clear version so the diction target is obvious.</li><li>Land Pss Tucka Tss Trio on a whispered clear sentence, then the same sentence sung.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Speak the pattern slowly in unison before any pitch or speed challenge.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Drop the warm-up consonants into a tricky lyric from the song.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = 'cc9dfea3-2fe9-445f-8e09-ff9ac7ff9f1c';

