    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Warm the face and diction with Lips Teeth Tongue. Say the sentence ‘The lips, the teeth, the tip of the tongue’.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Wakes facial muscles for clear diction</li><li>Improves articulation without forcing the voice</li><li>Strengthens tongue agility for consonants</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Standing or seated circle so everyone can see mouths. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Gather focus and watch the teacher model once in silence.</li><li>Say the sentence ‘The lips, the teeth, the tip of the tongue’.</li><li>Repeat together, then try once more with greater clarity.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>If the lip buzz in Lips Teeth Tongue won’t start, add a tiny voiced ‘b’ or ‘v’ underneath — never force dry lips.</li><li>For Lips Teeth Tongue, exaggerate tip-of-tongue consonants slowly first; only speed up when every syllable stays clear.</li><li>Close Lips Teeth Tongue by mouthing the pattern silently, then add voice — clarity first.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Speak the pattern slowly in unison before any pitch or speed challenge.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Drop the warm-up consonants into a tricky lyric from the song.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '3b4db96b-65ab-490d-a5d0-475a91cc63c1';

-- [Facial Warm-Ups / Diction] Travel Vocal Warm-Up
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Travel Vocal Warm-Up',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Warm the face and diction with Travel Vocal Warm-Up. ‘Travel vocal warm-up’.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Wakes facial muscles for clear diction</li><li>Improves articulation without forcing the voice</li><li>Makes warm-up playful so diction feels low-stakes</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Standing or seated circle so everyone can see mouths. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>‘Travel vocal warm-up’.</li><li>Teach the following parts and then put together: 1. ‘Mind the gap’ (dotted crotchet, quaver, crotchet, rest) 2. ‘Tickets please, umm, thank-you’ ( semiquaver, quaver, crotchet, crotchet, quaver, quaver) 3. ‘Travelling, hurrying’ (2 semiquavers, quaver, rest, 2 semiquavers, quaver, rest) 4. ‘I’m late, I’m late, for a very important date’.</li><li>(Quaver upbeat, dotted crotchet, quaver, dotted crotchet, 2 semiquavers, 4 semiquavers, crotchet).</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>For Travel Vocal Warm-Up, contrast a muddy version with a crystal-clear version so the diction target is obvious.</li><li>Land Travel Vocal Warm-Up on a whispered clear sentence, then the same sentence sung.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Speak the pattern slowly in unison before any pitch or speed challenge.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Drop the warm-up consonants into a tricky lyric from the song.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '32594946-638e-40d8-a5f6-f63fb9eeac3b';

-- [Facial Warm-Ups / Diction] School Rules Vocal Warm-Up
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'School Rules Vocal Warm-Up',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Warm the face and diction with School Rules Vocal Warm-Up. ‘School Rules vocal warm-up’: Teach the following sentences and then put together with suitable hand gestures for each one: 1.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Wakes facial muscles for clear diction</li><li>Improves articulation without forcing the voice</li><li>Makes warm-up playful so diction feels low-stakes</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Standing or seated circle so everyone can see mouths. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>‘School Rules vocal warm-up’: Teach the following sentences and then put together with suitable hand gestures for each one: 1. ‘Be quiet!’ (Crotchet, rest, crotchet, rest) 2. ‘I know the answer Miss.’ (Crotchet, 4 quavers, crotchet) 3. ‘What do ya think you’re doing!’ (Quaver, 2 semiquavers, 2 quavers, semiquaver dotted quaver) 4. ‘Gimme my ruler’ (2 semiquavers, quaver, crotchet, crotchet) - sound upset.</li><li>If ‘solo’ is called out one group will continue on their own, duet, two groups, everyone, silence, etc.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>For School Rules Vocal Warm-Up, contrast a muddy version with a crystal-clear version so the diction target is obvious.</li><li>Land School Rules Vocal Warm-Up on a whispered clear sentence, then the same sentence sung.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Speak the pattern slowly in unison before any pitch or speed challenge.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Drop the warm-up consonants into a tricky lyric from the song.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '4761350d-ce80-4375-b8d1-1a8b54b437a1';

-- [Facial Warm-Ups / Diction] Tongue Circles
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Tongue Circles',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Warm the face and diction with Tongue Circles. To exercise the tongue, place the tongue between your top teeth and closed lips.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Wakes facial muscles for clear diction</li><li>Improves articulation without forcing the voice</li><li>Strengthens tongue agility for consonants</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Standing or seated circle so everyone can see mouths. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>To exercise the tongue, place the tongue between your top teeth and closed lips.</li><li>Circle the tongue around your teeth 8 times clockwise, 8 times anti-clockwise, 4 times clockwise and 4 times anti-clockwise, 2 times and then once similarly.</li><li>Before doing this exercise say a tongue-twister such as ‘The tip of the tongue, the teeth and the lips, Aaaah’.</li><li>Repeat again once they have done the tongue exercise.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>If the lip buzz in Tongue Circles won’t start, add a tiny voiced ‘b’ or ‘v’ underneath — never force dry lips.</li><li>For Tongue Circles, exaggerate tip-of-tongue consonants slowly first; only speed up when every syllable stays clear.</li><li>Close Tongue Circles by mouthing the pattern silently, then add voice — clarity first.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Speak the pattern slowly in unison before any pitch or speed challenge.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Drop the warm-up consonants into a tricky lyric from the song.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '5c303368-9267-41af-809b-5f37fc1af467';

-- [Facial Warm-Ups / Diction] Italian Pasta Words
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Italian Pasta Words',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Warm the face and diction with Italian Pasta Words. Say the following Italian words which are all types of pasta: Macaroni, cannelloni; fettuccini, tortellini; capaletti or spaghetti.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Wakes facial muscles for clear diction</li><li>Improves articulation without forcing the voice</li><li>Makes warm-up playful so diction feels low-stakes</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Standing or seated circle so everyone can see mouths. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Say the following Italian words which are all types of pasta: Macaroni, cannelloni.</li><li>Fettuccini, tortellini.</li><li>Capaletti or spaghetti.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>For Italian Pasta Words, contrast a muddy version with a crystal-clear version so the diction target is obvious.</li><li>Close Italian Pasta Words by mouthing the pattern silently, then add voice — clarity first.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Speak the pattern slowly in unison before any pitch or speed challenge.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Drop the warm-up consonants into a tricky lyric from the song.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '101e43c4-052f-42e0-8bd1-c8c710dc52e2';

-- [Facial Warm-Ups / Diction] Amazing Present Open Throat
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Amazing Present Open Throat',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Warm the face and diction with Amazing Present Open Throat. Explain that for the voice not to sound ‘tight’ or strained the throat needs to be open and unconstricted.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Wakes facial muscles for clear diction</li><li>Improves articulation without forcing the voice</li><li>Makes warm-up playful so diction feels low-stakes</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Standing or seated circle so everyone can see mouths. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Explain that for the voice not to sound ‘tight’ or strained the throat needs to be open and unconstricted.</li><li>Imagine someone has just given you the most amazing present.</li><li>As you open the box you take a little inward breath making a silent ‘Ah!’ Feel the cold air at the back of the throat and a very open feeling.</li><li>Now try the same sensation without taking the in-breath, but still feeling that the throat is really open.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>For Amazing Present Open Throat, contrast a muddy version with a crystal-clear version so the diction target is obvious.</li><li>After Amazing Present Open Throat, spot one consonant that still smudges and drill only that word.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Speak the pattern slowly in unison before any pitch or speed challenge.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Drop the warm-up consonants into a tricky lyric from the song.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '0a68de99-f6a2-4893-b176-df9a80dad324';

-- [Facial Warm-Ups / Diction] Front and Back Tongue Consonants
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Front and Back Tongue Consonants',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Warm the face and diction with Front and Back Tongue Consonants. Make the sounds of different consonants and feel where the effort is in the tongue eg ‘tttt’ or ‘dddd’ at the front tip of the tongue; ‘gggg’ or….</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Wakes facial muscles for clear diction</li><li>Improves articulation without forcing the voice</li><li>Strengthens tongue agility for consonants</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Standing or seated circle so everyone can see mouths. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Make the sounds of different consonants and feel where the effort is in the tongue eg ‘tttt’ or ‘dddd’ at the front tip of the tongue.</li><li>‘gggg’ or ‘kkkk’ at the back of the tongue.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>For Front and Back Tongue Consonants, exaggerate tip-of-tongue consonants slowly first; only speed up when every syllable stays clear.</li><li>For Front and Back Tongue Consonants, contrast a muddy version with a crystal-clear version so the diction target is obvious.</li><li>After Front and Back Tongue Consonants, spot one consonant that still smudges and drill only that word.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Speak the pattern slowly in unison before any pitch or speed challenge.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Drop the warm-up consonants into a tricky lyric from the song.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = 'e463c8a8-981a-4386-bf9d-e1ec78d55e92';

-- [Facial Warm-Ups / Diction] Small Face Big Face
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Small Face Big Face',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Warm the face and diction with Small Face Big Face. Make a small face (screw up all the muscles into the middle).</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Wakes facial muscles for clear diction</li><li>Improves articulation without forcing the voice</li><li>Makes warm-up playful so diction feels low-stakes</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Standing or seated circle so everyone can see mouths. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Make a small face (screw up all the muscles into the middle).</li><li>On a clap, make the face as big as possible (wide open mouth, eyebrows lifted etc.).</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>For Small Face Big Face, contrast a muddy version with a crystal-clear version so the diction target is obvious.</li><li>After Small Face Big Face, spot one consonant that still smudges and drill only that word.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Speak the pattern slowly in unison before any pitch or speed challenge.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Raise the tempo or move the pattern up by semitones while keeping every consonant.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Drop the warm-up consonants into a tricky lyric from the song.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '31185bbe-3b81-4246-8f71-a29cb46100ed';

-- [Facial Warm-Ups / Diction] Faces Morphing
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Faces Morphing',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Warm the face and diction with Faces Morphing. Faces: morphing!.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Wakes facial muscles for clear diction</li><li>Improves articulation without forcing the voice</li><li>Makes warm-up playful so diction feels low-stakes</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Standing or seated circle so everyone can see mouths. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Faces: morphing!</li><li>Think of contrasting facial expressions – happy/miserable, laughing/crying, shocked/sleepy etc.</li><li>Imagine the face is a piece of rubber being slowly transformed in slow motion, from the first expression to the second.</li><li>Change expressions over 8 beats to get really exaggerated expressions.</li><li>And then quicken to 4 and then 2 beats to get quick, clear reactions.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>For Faces Morphing, contrast a muddy version with a crystal-clear version so the diction target is obvious.</li><li>Land Faces Morphing on a whispered clear sentence, then the same sentence sung.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Speak the pattern slowly in unison before any pitch or speed challenge.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Drop the warm-up consonants into a tricky lyric from the song.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '297a90e7-6ef3-474e-ae55-6951350d4344';

-- [Facial Warm-Ups / Diction] Jaw Hinge Check
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Jaw Hinge Check',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Warm the face and diction with Jaw Hinge Check. Place fingers on the ‘hinge’ of the jaw and feel the degree of movement when opening and closing the mouth.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Wakes facial muscles for clear diction</li><li>Improves articulation without forcing the voice</li><li>Makes warm-up playful so diction feels low-stakes</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Standing or seated circle so everyone can see mouths. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Gather focus and watch the teacher model once in silence.</li><li>Place fingers on the ‘hinge’ of the jaw and feel the degree of movement when opening and closing the mouth.</li><li>Repeat together, then try once more with greater clarity.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>For Jaw Hinge Check, contrast a muddy version with a crystal-clear version so the diction target is obvious.</li><li>Land Jaw Hinge Check on a whispered clear sentence, then the same sentence sung.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Speak the pattern slowly in unison before any pitch or speed challenge.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Drop the warm-up consonants into a tricky lyric from the song.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '7b16088c-c6a3-41e8-b2f1-f88d1484c9bd';

-- [Facial Warm-Ups / Diction] Busy Bee
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Busy Bee',
    time = 2,
    description = '<p>Warm the face and diction with Busy Bee. Put finger and thumb together and be a busy bee buzzing around.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Wakes facial muscles for clear diction</li><li>Improves articulation without forcing the voice</li><li>Makes warm-up playful so diction feels low-stakes</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Standing or seated circle so everyone can see mouths. No props needed. About 2 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Put finger and thumb together and be a busy bee buzzing around.</li><li>When it is placed on the floor they are silent.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>For Busy Bee, contrast a muddy version with a crystal-clear version so the diction target is obvious.</li><li>Close Busy Bee by mouthing the pattern silently, then add voice — clarity first.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Speak the pattern slowly in unison before any pitch or speed challenge.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Drop the warm-up consonants into a tricky lyric from the song.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
