-- [Pitch Exercises] Fluffy Floppy Puppy
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Fluffy Floppy Puppy',
    time = 3,
    description = '<p>Explore pitch and listening with Fluffy Floppy Puppy. Sing down the scale with the words ‘Fluffy, floppy, puppy’.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Explores pitch range safely and playfully</li><li>Builds listening and pitch-matching accuracy</li><li>Smooths register transitions without pushing</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Standing circle or open rows facing the leader. Optional: piano or pitch reference. About 3 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Gather focus and watch the teacher model once in silence.</li><li>Sing down the scale with the words ‘Fluffy, floppy, puppy’.</li><li>Repeat together, then try once more with greater clarity.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>Give a clear starting pitch each repeat of Fluffy Floppy Puppy; if top notes shout, narrow to a gentle ‘ng’ slide first.</li><li>On Fluffy Floppy Puppy, isolate the hardest pitch gesture once, then rejoin the full pattern at a slower tempo.</li><li>Resolve Fluffy Floppy Puppy onto a comfortable mid-range hum before repertoire.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Narrow the pitch range and let the class echo the leader phrase by phrase.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Improvise a four-beat answer after the set pattern, still matching the leader’s vowel.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '97d6a556-b674-4ce1-88a9-10e2a8a2b62d';

-- [Pitch Exercises] Planet Ning and Zing
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Planet Ning and Zing',
    time = 3,
    description = '<p>Explore pitch and listening with Planet Ning and Zing. In pairs, the first person to go imagines they are an alien from the planet Ning.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Explores pitch range safely and playfully</li><li>Builds listening and pitch-matching accuracy</li><li>Smooths register transitions without pushing</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Standing circle or open rows facing the leader. Optional: piano or pitch reference. About 3 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>In pairs, the first person to go imagines they are an alien from the planet Ning.</li><li>They speak Ning language which goes like this – demonstrate.</li><li>This person is going to tell the other person all about their weekend, when they went to see a trampolining competition where all the competitors jumped off a very high cliff, landed on a trampoline at the bottom, one of these super trampolines which can bounce you miles into the air and then drop you back down again.</li><li>You found all this very exciting, particularly the way they jumped very high and then very low.</li><li>Then switch over, with the other person being an alien from the planet ‘Zing’.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>On Planet Ning and Zing, isolate the hardest pitch gesture once, then rejoin the full pattern at a slower tempo.</li><li>After Planet Ning and Zing, sing the first phrase of the song on ‘ng’ using the same freedom.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Narrow the pitch range and let the class echo the leader phrase by phrase.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Improvise a four-beat answer after the set pattern, still matching the leader’s vowel.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = 'ffe10137-6862-4cfd-ada7-dbe84cd05606';

-- [Pitch Exercises] Exploring Vowel Sounds
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Exploring Vowel Sounds',
    time = 3,
    description = '<p>Explore pitch and listening with Exploring Vowel Sounds. Exploring vowel sounds: The English language is a complicated language to sing in because the vowel sounds are not all ‘pure’ but many go from one….</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Explores pitch range safely and playfully</li><li>Builds listening and pitch-matching accuracy</li><li>Smooths register transitions without pushing</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Standing circle or open rows facing the leader. Optional: piano or pitch reference. About 3 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Exploring vowel sounds: The English language is a complicated language to sing in because the vowel sounds are not all ‘pure’ but many go from one sound to another in what is called a ‘dipthong’.</li><li>Take each of the 5 vowel sounds and work out which of them is pure and which is not: A = ‘eh-ee’ E = ‘ee’ I = ‘ah-ee’ O = ‘oh-oo’ U = ‘ee-oo’ When we sing, we want to move to the second of the two sounds as late as possible, otherwise the resulting sound is a very quick, closed sound, which is harder to sing.</li><li>Practice this with the word ‘high’ – Change quickly: ‘Ha-eee’; Change slowly: ‘Haaaa-ee’.</li><li>Asks them to identify which one sounds better.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>On vowel changes in Exploring Vowel Sounds, keep the jaw released; spot-check one row if sounds collapse into a muffled schwa.</li><li>On Exploring Vowel Sounds, isolate the hardest pitch gesture once, then rejoin the full pattern at a slower tempo.</li><li>Finish Exploring Vowel Sounds descending into speaking range so nobody carries tension upward.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Narrow the pitch range and let the class echo the leader phrase by phrase.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Improvise a four-beat answer after the set pattern, still matching the leader’s vowel.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '39f292da-ae8a-4fc8-8546-d22c5ed837ef';

-- [Pitch Exercises] Child-Chosen Vowel and Pitch
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Child-Chosen Vowel and Pitch',
    time = 3,
    description = '<p>Explore pitch and listening with Child-Chosen Vowel and Pitch. Ask a child for a vowel sound and another child for a pitch and sing a long note on this note for 8 beats.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Explores pitch range safely and playfully</li><li>Builds listening and pitch-matching accuracy</li><li>Smooths register transitions without pushing</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Standing circle or open rows facing the leader. Optional: piano or pitch reference. About 3 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Ask a child for a vowel sound and another child for a pitch and sing a long note on this note for 8 beats.</li><li>Select another 2 children for a note and vowel sound and repeat.</li><li>Ask the group how they can make this sound more interesting – move onto the note a 3rd above.</li><li>Sing two pitches, moving up on my hand signal.</li><li>Divide the group in half and have one half starting on one note and the other half the other note.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>On vowel changes in Child-Chosen Vowel and Pitch, keep the jaw released; spot-check one row if sounds collapse into a muffled schwa.</li><li>On Child-Chosen Vowel and Pitch, isolate the hardest pitch gesture once, then rejoin the full pattern at a slower tempo.</li><li>After Child-Chosen Vowel and Pitch, sing the first phrase of the song on ‘ng’ using the same freedom.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Narrow the pitch range and let the class echo the leader phrase by phrase.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Improvise a four-beat answer after the set pattern, still matching the leader’s vowel.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '78457032-0ddd-4e1a-916d-e535f174fbef';

-- [Pitch Exercises] Sing 5 Pitches
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Sing 5 Pitches',
    time = 3,
    description = '<p>Explore pitch and listening with Sing 5 Pitches. Sing ‘Sing’ going up and down 5 pitches, moving on to the ‘ng’ sound straightaway.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Explores pitch range safely and playfully</li><li>Builds listening and pitch-matching accuracy</li><li>Smooths register transitions without pushing</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Standing circle or open rows facing the leader. Optional: piano or pitch reference. About 3 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Sing ‘Sing’ going up and down 5 pitches, moving on to the ‘ng’ sound straightaway.</li><li>Then the dominant and tonic notes again.</li><li>Move up a semitone.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>Aim for a continuous buzz in Sing 5 Pitches with almost no air leak; if tone thins, drop the range and widen the smile slightly.</li><li>Give a clear starting pitch each repeat of Sing 5 Pitches; if top notes shout, narrow to a gentle ‘ng’ slide first.</li><li>Finish Sing 5 Pitches descending into speaking range so nobody carries tension upward.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Narrow the pitch range and let the class echo the leader phrase by phrase.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Improvise a four-beat answer after the set pattern, still matching the leader’s vowel.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '2d82750a-3698-4f83-97d4-ec1c0d996b3c';

-- [Pitch Exercises] Voice Play Copycat
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Voice Play Copycat',
    time = 3,
    description = '<p>Explore pitch and listening with Voice Play Copycat. Voice play where the group copy sounds started by the leader.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Explores pitch range safely and playfully</li><li>Builds listening and pitch-matching accuracy</li><li>Smooths register transitions without pushing</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Standing circle or open rows facing the leader. Optional: piano or pitch reference. About 3 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Voice play where the group copy sounds started by the leader.</li><li>A quick ‘Whoa’ sliding up, ‘Whee’ coming down, ‘Aah’ (a sigh), ‘mm’ (C E), ‘Did you?’ (quite high pitched voice), ‘Aooh’ sliding down, ‘Hey!’ in a deep voice, ‘Ho!’.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>Aim for a continuous buzz in Voice Play Copycat with almost no air leak; if tone thins, drop the range and widen the smile slightly.</li><li>On Voice Play Copycat, isolate the hardest pitch gesture once, then rejoin the full pattern at a slower tempo.</li><li>Resolve Voice Play Copycat onto a comfortable mid-range hum before repertoire.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Narrow the pitch range and let the class echo the leader phrase by phrase.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Improvise a four-beat answer after the set pattern, still matching the leader’s vowel.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '5593f7db-e292-40cd-b553-ee96884eb729';

-- [Pitch Exercises] Puppy in a Cupboard / Monkey
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Puppy in a Cupboard / Monkey',
    time = 3,
    description = '<p>Explore pitch and listening with Puppy in a Cupboard / Monkey. Imagine you have a very cute little puppy which someone has locked in a cupboard by mistake.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Explores pitch range safely and playfully</li><li>Builds listening and pitch-matching accuracy</li><li>Encourages bold but controlled vocal play</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Standing circle or open rows facing the leader. Optional: piano or pitch reference. About 3 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Imagine you have a very cute little puppy which someone has locked in a cupboard by mistake.</li><li>What would it sound like?</li><li>Then pretend to be a monkey ‘Ooh, ooh, ooh’ in a deepish voice.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>Alternate sound effects in Puppy in a Cupboard / Monkey with a recovery breath so the class doesn’t get dizzy.</li><li>On Puppy in a Cupboard / Monkey, isolate the hardest pitch gesture once, then rejoin the full pattern at a slower tempo.</li><li>Finish Puppy in a Cupboard / Monkey descending into speaking range so nobody carries tension upward.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Narrow the pitch range and let the class echo the leader phrase by phrase.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Widen the range, add a dynamic swell, or run as a short canon.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Improvise a four-beat answer after the set pattern, still matching the leader’s vowel.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = 'fda014a8-b766-41bb-9da9-8f05cd967657';

-- [Pitch Exercises] Interval Song Starters
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Interval Song Starters',
    time = 3,
    description = '<p>Explore pitch and listening with Interval Song Starters. Explain how the gaps between notes are called ‘intervals’.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Explores pitch range safely and playfully</li><li>Builds listening and pitch-matching accuracy</li><li>Smooths register transitions without pushing</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Standing circle or open rows facing the leader. Optional: piano or pitch reference. About 3 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Explain how the gaps between notes are called ‘intervals’.</li><li>Teach the following intervals using the opening of the following well-known songs: Happy birthday to you = 2nd Once in royal David’s City = 3rd A – way in a manger = 4th Twinkle twinkle little star = 5th My bon – nie lies over the ocean = 6th Somewhere o – ver the rainbow = 7th Some – where over the rainbow = 8th Play around with some of these opening phrases of the songs to see how well they can sing the intervals.</li><li>Give a starting note each time and see if the group can sing the next note of the interval given.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>On Interval Song Starters, isolate the hardest pitch gesture once, then rejoin the full pattern at a slower tempo.</li><li>After Interval Song Starters, sing the first phrase of the song on ‘ng’ using the same freedom.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Narrow the pitch range and let the class echo the leader phrase by phrase.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Improvise a four-beat answer after the set pattern, still matching the leader’s vowel.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '84738de1-876b-4bf2-81f9-a59c9b997587';

-- [Pitch Exercises] Witch and Hot Potato
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Witch and Hot Potato',
    time = 3,
    description = '<p>Explore pitch and listening with Witch and Hot Potato. Say the sentence ‘I am a nasty witch’ sounding like an evil witch.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Explores pitch range safely and playfully</li><li>Builds listening and pitch-matching accuracy</li><li>Smooths register transitions without pushing</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Standing circle or open rows facing the leader. Optional: piano or pitch reference. About 3 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Say the sentence ‘I am a nasty witch’ sounding like an evil witch.</li><li>Sing the phrase on a monotone.</li><li>Then say the sentence ‘I’ve got a piece of hot potato on my tongue’ in a posh voice.</li><li>Sing this phrase on a monotone.</li><li>Remove the words and sing to ‘Aah’ sounding like a witch then with a hot potato sound.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>For Witch and Hot Potato, exaggerate tip-of-tongue consonants slowly first; only speed up when every syllable stays clear.</li><li>On Witch and Hot Potato, isolate the hardest pitch gesture once, then rejoin the full pattern at a slower tempo.</li><li>Finish Witch and Hot Potato descending into speaking range so nobody carries tension upward.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Narrow the pitch range and let the class echo the leader phrase by phrase.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Improvise a four-beat answer after the set pattern, still matching the leader’s vowel.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '8eb9d8cc-524e-41f1-8dad-6938cab34fd1';

-- [Pitch Exercises] Marching Tune Call and Response
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Marching Tune Call and Response',
    time = 3,
    description = '<p>Explore pitch and listening with Marching Tune Call and Response. Speak the following phrases using different voices eg.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Explores pitch range safely and playfully</li><li>Builds listening and pitch-matching accuracy</li><li>Smooths register transitions without pushing</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Standing circle or open rows facing the leader. Optional: piano or pitch reference. About 3 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Speak the following phrases using different voices eg. sounding bored, angry or posh and in the form of call &amp; response.</li><li>I don’t want to sing today, but I will sing anyway.</li><li>And if what I sing sounds good, then I’m singing as I should!</li><li>Stand up straight, don’t look at the floor!</li><li>Lookin’ good and feelin’ fine!</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>On Marching Tune Call and Response, isolate the hardest pitch gesture once, then rejoin the full pattern at a slower tempo.</li><li>Resolve Marching Tune Call and Response onto a comfortable mid-range hum before repertoire.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Narrow the pitch range and let the class echo the leader phrase by phrase.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Improvise a four-beat answer after the set pattern, still matching the leader’s vowel.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '132b93e8-79d0-41fb-b12b-58572ba4cf5e';

-- [Pitch Exercises] Gonna Sing This Song
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Gonna Sing This Song',
    time = 3,
    description = '<p>Explore pitch and listening with Gonna Sing This Song. Using the same tune as above sing these words, changing the type of voice used each time eg.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Explores pitch range safely and playfully</li><li>Builds listening and pitch-matching accuracy</li><li>Smooths register transitions without pushing</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Standing circle or open rows facing the leader. Optional: piano or pitch reference. About 3 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Using the same tune as above sing these words, changing the type of voice used each time eg. normal.</li><li>Nasal.</li><li>As if in slow motion moving as if they are up in space.</li><li>Fast and with a high, nasal sound: We are gonna sing this song, gonna sing this all day long.</li><li>This song has got a kickin’ beat, we’re clicking our fingers and tapping our feet.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>On Gonna Sing This Song, isolate the hardest pitch gesture once, then rejoin the full pattern at a slower tempo.</li><li>Finish Gonna Sing This Song descending into speaking range so nobody carries tension upward.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Narrow the pitch range and let the class echo the leader phrase by phrase.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Improvise a four-beat answer after the set pattern, still matching the leader’s vowel.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = 'd80b1ad8-f840-4ee5-838f-be3cd91d31c7';

-- [Pitch Exercises] Chest Voice to Head Voice
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Chest Voice to Head Voice',
    time = 3,
    description = '<p>Explore pitch and listening with Chest Voice to Head Voice. Explain that the voice has two main registers or sections.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Explores pitch range safely and playfully</li><li>Builds listening and pitch-matching accuracy</li><li>Smooths register transitions without pushing</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Standing circle or open rows facing the leader. Optional: piano or pitch reference. About 3 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Explain that the voice has two main registers or sections.</li><li>The lower part of the voice, often known as the ‘chest’ voice and the higher part of the voice, the ‘head’ voice.</li><li>As the voice goes higher it needs to make an adjustment, rather like changing gear on a car.</li><li>Vocally, what needs to happen is that the larynx needs to tilt forward.</li><li>To make this happen, ask them to imitate the sound of a puppy whining outside a window, wanting you to let him in.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>On Chest Voice to Head Voice, isolate the hardest pitch gesture once, then rejoin the full pattern at a slower tempo.</li><li>Resolve Chest Voice to Head Voice onto a comfortable mid-range hum before repertoire.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Narrow the pitch range and let the class echo the leader phrase by phrase.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Improvise a four-beat answer after the set pattern, still matching the leader’s vowel.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '8eef339a-1028-4254-a120-ecc88373383b';

-- [Pitch Exercises] Yoo Hoo / It’s Me
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Yoo Hoo / It’s Me',
    time = 3,
    description = '<p>Explore pitch and listening with Yoo Hoo / It’s Me. Play ‘Yoo hoo!’ Ask them to imagine they’ve just spotted their best friend over the other side of the road.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Explores pitch range safely and playfully</li><li>Builds listening and pitch-matching accuracy</li><li>Smooths register transitions without pushing</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Standing circle or open rows facing the leader. Optional: piano or pitch reference. About 3 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Play ‘Yoo hoo!’ Ask them to imagine they’ve just spotted their best friend over the other side of the road.</li><li>Say altogether …. ‘Yoo hoo!’ with their arm in the air.</li><li>Then answer ‘It’s me’ in their deepest voice with their hand on their chest.</li><li>Repeat with the other hand.</li><li>Point out that the top ‘Yoo’ can be turned into a proper note.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>On Yoo Hoo / It’s Me, isolate the hardest pitch gesture once, then rejoin the full pattern at a slower tempo.</li><li>Finish Yoo Hoo / It’s Me descending into speaking range so nobody carries tension upward.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Narrow the pitch range and let the class echo the leader phrase by phrase.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Improvise a four-beat answer after the set pattern, still matching the leader’s vowel.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '25fd1a69-5342-4ba4-9510-6a7d01b3f974';

-- [Pitch Exercises] Missing Note 5-Note Scale
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Missing Note 5-Note Scale',
    time = 3,
    description = '<p>Explore pitch and listening with Missing Note 5-Note Scale. Sing up and down a 5 note scale, holding onto the 5th note for 3 beats, then come down.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Explores pitch range safely and playfully</li><li>Builds listening and pitch-matching accuracy</li><li>Smooths register transitions without pushing</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Standing circle or open rows facing the leader. Optional: piano or pitch reference. About 3 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Sing up and down a 5 note scale, holding onto the 5th note for 3 beats, then come down.</li><li>Try missing out a note but make sure you hear the missing note in your head with your thinking voice.</li><li>Then miss out two notes and sing up and down the scale again.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>Give a clear starting pitch each repeat of Missing Note 5-Note Scale; if top notes shout, narrow to a gentle ‘ng’ slide first.</li><li>On Missing Note 5-Note Scale, isolate the hardest pitch gesture once, then rejoin the full pattern at a slower tempo.</li><li>After Missing Note 5-Note Scale, sing the first phrase of the song on ‘ng’ using the same freedom.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Narrow the pitch range and let the class echo the leader phrase by phrase.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Improvise a four-beat answer after the set pattern, still matching the leader’s vowel.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '80688862-404d-4626-8517-f54203ad700a';

-- [Pitch Exercises] Going Up the Stairs
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Going Up the Stairs',
    time = 3,
    description = '<p>Explore pitch and listening with Going Up the Stairs. Sing up the scale ‘1 2 3 4 5 4 3 2 1’ to the words ‘Going up the stairs and coming down’, with the scale being sung a semitone higher each time.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Explores pitch range safely and playfully</li><li>Builds listening and pitch-matching accuracy</li><li>Smooths register transitions without pushing</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Standing circle or open rows facing the leader. Optional: piano or pitch reference. About 3 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Sing up the scale ‘1 2 3 4 5 4 3 2 1’ to the words ‘Going up the stairs and coming down’, with the scale being sung a semitone higher each time.</li><li>Change the words to ‘Oo’, ‘Na’, ‘Mah’, ‘Bah’ etc.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>Give a clear starting pitch each repeat of Going Up the Stairs; if top notes shout, narrow to a gentle ‘ng’ slide first.</li><li>On Going Up the Stairs, isolate the hardest pitch gesture once, then rejoin the full pattern at a slower tempo.</li><li>After Going Up the Stairs, sing the first phrase of the song on ‘ng’ using the same freedom.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Narrow the pitch range and let the class echo the leader phrase by phrase.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Improvise a four-beat answer after the set pattern, still matching the leader’s vowel.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '74028edd-bc0e-49fb-a118-b987f242126a';

-- [Pitch Exercises] Yo Yo
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Yo Yo',
    time = 3,
    description = '<p>Explore pitch and listening with Yo Yo. ‘Yo Yo’.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Explores pitch range safely and playfully</li><li>Builds listening and pitch-matching accuracy</li><li>Smooths register transitions without pushing</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Standing circle or open rows facing the leader. Optional: piano or pitch reference. About 3 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>‘Yo Yo’.</li><li>Have an imaginary yo-yo.</li><li>Say ‘Yo Yo’ sliding down and back up with your voice on the first ‘Yo’ with the 2nd ‘Yo’ being shorter at the top.</li><li>Then do tricks with your yo-yo eg. flinging it out in front of you and it comes back.</li><li>Around the world in a circle.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>On Yo Yo, isolate the hardest pitch gesture once, then rejoin the full pattern at a slower tempo.</li><li>Resolve Yo Yo onto a comfortable mid-range hum before repertoire.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Narrow the pitch range and let the class echo the leader phrase by phrase.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Improvise a four-beat answer after the set pattern, still matching the leader’s vowel.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '09620812-2a21-4114-9642-a993018e9776';

-- [Pitch Exercises] Fly Buzz Splat Gulp
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Fly Buzz Splat Gulp',
    time = 3,
    description = '<p>Explore pitch and listening with Fly Buzz Splat Gulp. Make the sound of a fly buzzing and the children follow this fly as you move your hand up and down.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Explores pitch range safely and playfully</li><li>Builds listening and pitch-matching accuracy</li><li>Smooths register transitions without pushing</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Standing circle or open rows facing the leader. Optional: piano or pitch reference. About 3 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Make the sound of a fly buzzing and the children follow this fly as you move your hand up and down.</li><li>Then you catch the fly with a clap and say ‘Splat’.</li><li>Then you open it and go ‘Huh?’ Then you eat it, with a ‘gulp, gulp’ and then you say ‘Mmm’.</li><li>Then you realise you’ve eaten a fly and you go ‘Yuk!’.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>On Fly Buzz Splat Gulp, isolate the hardest pitch gesture once, then rejoin the full pattern at a slower tempo.</li><li>Resolve Fly Buzz Splat Gulp onto a comfortable mid-range hum before repertoire.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Narrow the pitch range and let the class echo the leader phrase by phrase.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Improvise a four-beat answer after the set pattern, still matching the leader’s vowel.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = 'd68ca2ba-5562-42c8-8ba4-86454d33c348';

-- [Pitch Exercises] Ghost Exercise
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Ghost Exercise',
    time = 3,
    description = '<p>Explore pitch and listening with Ghost Exercise. The ‘Ghost Exercise’ involves using an ‘oo’ sound to swoop the voice up and down from the very highest point they can squeak to the very lowest….</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Explores pitch range safely and playfully</li><li>Builds listening and pitch-matching accuracy</li><li>Smooths register transitions without pushing</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Standing circle or open rows facing the leader. Optional: piano or pitch reference. About 3 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Gather focus and watch the teacher model once in silence.</li><li>The ‘Ghost Exercise’ involves using an ‘oo’ sound to swoop the voice up and down from the very highest point they can squeak to the very lowest point they can reach.</li><li>Repeat together, then try once more with greater clarity.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>On Ghost Exercise, isolate the hardest pitch gesture once, then rejoin the full pattern at a slower tempo.</li><li>Finish Ghost Exercise descending into speaking range so nobody carries tension upward.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Narrow the pitch range and let the class echo the leader phrase by phrase.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Improvise a four-beat answer after the set pattern, still matching the leader’s vowel.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = '6609fe88-d84f-415d-a31c-cdabe1768862';

-- [Pitch Exercises] Voice Drawing Shapes
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Voice Drawing Shapes',
    time = 3,
    description = '<p>Explore pitch and listening with Voice Drawing Shapes. Draw on the IWB various shapes (vocal sound pictures) – curved lines etc.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Explores pitch range safely and playfully</li><li>Builds listening and pitch-matching accuracy</li><li>Smooths register transitions without pushing</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Standing circle or open rows facing the leader. Optional: piano or pitch reference. About 3 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Draw on the IWB various shapes (vocal sound pictures) – curved lines etc. – point to a shape and trace the line.</li><li>Can they make the shape of the lines with their voices?</li><li>Sing to ‘oo’, ‘noo’, ‘ee’ or ‘nee’.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>On Voice Drawing Shapes, isolate the hardest pitch gesture once, then rejoin the full pattern at a slower tempo.</li><li>After Voice Drawing Shapes, sing the first phrase of the song on ‘ng’ using the same freedom.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Narrow the pitch range and let the class echo the leader phrase by phrase.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Improvise a four-beat answer after the set pattern, still matching the leader’s vowel.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE id = 'dd141aa4-1bda-403f-bb83-d6c7ac27b244';

-- [Pitch Exercises] Octave Leaps
UPDATE activities
SET activity = 'Octave Leaps',
    time = 3,
    description = '<p>Explore pitch and listening with Octave Leaps. Sing up the scale on one syllable followed by octave leaps.</p><p><strong>Why it works</strong></p><ul><li>Explores pitch range safely and playfully</li><li>Builds listening and pitch-matching accuracy</li><li>Smooths register transitions without pushing</li></ul>',
    activity_text = '<p><strong>Setup</strong></p><p>Standing circle or open rows facing the leader. Optional: piano or pitch reference. About 3 minutes.</p><p><strong>How to do it</strong></p><ol><li>Gather focus and watch the teacher model once in silence.</li><li>Sing up the scale on one syllable followed by octave leaps.</li><li>Repeat together, then try once more with greater clarity.</li></ol><p><strong>Teacher tips</strong></p><ul><li>Give a clear starting pitch each repeat of Octave Leaps; if top notes shout, narrow to a gentle ‘ng’ slide first.</li><li>On Octave Leaps, isolate the hardest pitch gesture once, then rejoin the full pattern at a slower tempo.</li><li>Resolve Octave Leaps onto a comfortable mid-range hum before repertoire.</li></ul><p><strong>Make it easier</strong></p><p>Narrow the pitch range and let the class echo the leader phrase by phrase.</p><p><strong>Make it harder</strong></p><p>Siren through a wider interval, then land softly on a sung ‘ah’.</p><p><strong>Extension</strong></p><p>Improvise a four-beat answer after the set pattern, still matching the leader’s vowel.</p>',
    updated_at = NOW()
